# Logging Out with Sessions

Logging in hands out a badge. Logging out has to take it back, and there are **two copies** of the arrangement to undo:

1. The server's record of the session.
2. The cookie in the browser.

Doing only one of them leaves a mess, so we will do both. This reading's snapshot is `auth-demo/05-logout/`: stage 4 plus one endpoint and one button.

## The Button

```{walkthrough}
title: The client side of leaving
step: A button in the welcome block
file: auth-demo/05-logout/static/index.html
region: user-info
The welcome block from the last reading grows a Log out button. It lives inside `user-info`, so it only ever appears to someone who is logged in.

step: The click handler
file: auth-demo/05-logout/static/index.html
region: script
`checkSession` is unchanged from stage 4 except for its `else` branch, which the last section of this reading comes back to. The new part is at the bottom.
@"logoutButton.addEventListener": The callback is `async` so it can `await` the fetch: do not reload until the server has actually heard the request.
@"fetch('/logout'": `POST`, not `GET`. Either would work, and logging out changes something on the server, which is what `POST` is for. It also leaves room to send data with the request some day.
@"window.location.reload": Reload regardless of the answer: there is no check of `response.ok` here. There is very little a failed logout could mean that a reload does not sort out, and leaving someone looking at a logged-in page when they asked to leave is the worse failure.
```

## The Endpoint

```{walkthrough}
title: POST /logout
file: auth-demo/05-logout/server.js
region: logout
The shortest endpoint on the server, and it does both halves of the undoing.
@"cookies.sessionId": Identify the badge the same way `/me` does: parse the cookies, take the session ID.
@"delete sessions[sessionId]": `delete` removes the key from the object. After this the ID means nothing: a request carrying it fails the `sessions[sessionId]` check in `/me` exactly like a made-up one.
@"Max-Age=0": The cookie-deletion technique from the cookies reading: an empty value, expiring immediately. The browser is told the cookie is already dead, and discards it. Note the response is still a `200`; asking to leave and succeeding is not an error.
```

## Why Both

It is tempting to think that clearing the cookie is enough, since without the cookie the browser cannot identify itself. Two reasons that is not enough.

**A session that is not deleted stays valid.** If anyone captured that session ID earlier, through a shared machine, a logged proxy, or a script that got at it, they can keep using it. The user believes they logged out; the badge is still live. Deleting the server record is what actually ends the session:

```{node autorun}
const sessions = {
  'badge-1': { email: 'soney@umich.edu' },
  'badge-2': { email: 'grace@example.com' }
};

// Suppose someone copied this ID off a shared machine earlier today.
const stolenId = 'badge-1';

// The real user logs out: the server deletes its half of the arrangement.
delete sessions[stolenId];

console.log('what the stolen ID is worth now: ' + sessions[stolenId]);
console.log('sessions the server still holds: ' + Object.keys(sessions).length);
```

Comment out the `delete` line and run the cell again: the stolen badge stays usable forever, whatever the real user's browser did with its cookie.

**And the entry would never go away.** Every login adds to `sessions` and nothing would remove anything, so a long-running server accumulates dead sessions for as long as it runs.

Deleting the cookie is a courtesy to the browser. Deleting the session is the logout.

## Showing the Right Navigation

One loose end from the previous reading: the home page showed the Register and Log In links even to someone already logged in. The fix is to wrap them:

```{walkthrough}
title: Two navigations, one shown
file: auth-demo/05-logout/static/index.html
region: guest-nav
The links from stage 4, now inside a wrapper that starts hidden, exactly like `user-info` below it.
@"display:none": Both blocks start hidden, and `checkSession` reveals exactly one: `user-info` in the `if`, this block in the `else`. Starting both hidden is deliberate: whichever way the answer from `/me` goes, the page never flashes the wrong navigation while it waits.
```

## That Is the Whole Thing

Registration, login, hashed passwords, sessions, and logout, built by hand across five stages. It is worth knowing what each piece does, and worth repeating the recommendation from every video in this run: **in production, use a library.** The next reading does exactly that, rebuilding this same server on Express and Passport, and the pages in `static/` will not change at all.

## Running It Yourself

From the `auth-demo` folder (stop the previous stage with `Ctrl+C` first):

```
$ node 05-logout/server.js
Listening on port 3000
```

1. Fresh stage, fresh `users.json`: register, then log in. The home page now greets you, shows a Log out button, and the Register and Log In links are gone.
2. Check the cookies in the developer tools (Application > Cookies): there is the `sessionId`.
3. Click Log out. The page reloads as a guest, with the links back. Look at the cookies again: `sessionId` is gone, expired by `Max-Age=0`.
4. For the other half of the story, log in again, but this time restart the *server* instead of clicking the button. Same visible result, opposite mechanism: the button deletes the server's record and expires the cookie; the restart wiped the records while the cookie survived, uselessly.

## Exercises

1. Complete the logout handler.

    ```{node}
    const sessions = {
      'abc-123': { email: 'grace@example.com' },
      'xyz-987': { email: 'ada@example.com' }
    };

    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleLogout(cookies, response) {
      // TODO: remove this session from the server's records, then answer 200
      // with a Set-Cookie header that expires the sessionId cookie
      // immediately, and the body 'Logged out'.
    }

    const response = makeFakeResponse();
    handleLogout({ sessionId: 'abc-123' }, response);

    console.log(response.statusCode + ' ' + response.body);
    console.log(sessions);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const sessions = {
      'abc-123': { email: 'grace@example.com' },
      'xyz-987': { email: 'ada@example.com' }
    };

    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleLogout(cookies, response) {
      const sessionId = cookies.sessionId;

      if (sessions[sessionId]) {
        delete sessions[sessionId];
      }

      response.writeHead(200, {
        'Set-Cookie': 'sessionId=; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict; Max-Age=0'
      });
      response.end('Logged out');
    }

    const response = makeFakeResponse();
    handleLogout({ sessionId: 'abc-123' }, response);

    console.log(response.statusCode + ' ' + response.body);
    console.log(sessions);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap('abc-123' in sessions).should('equal', false).run('Remove the session from `sessions` with `delete`', 'Deleted the session');
    wrap('xyz-987' in sessions).should('equal', true).run('Only the session being logged out should be removed, not the others', 'Left other sessions alone');
    wrap(response.statusCode + '|' + response.body).should('equal', '200|Logged out').run('Answer `200` with the body `Logged out`', 'Answered the request');
    wrap(/Max-Age=0/.test(response.headers && response.headers['Set-Cookie'])).should('equal', true).run('Send a `Set-Cookie` header with `Max-Age=0`, which tells the browser the cookie has expired', 'Expired the cookie');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleLogout({}, r); return r.statusCode; })()).should('equal', 200).run('A logout request with no session cookie should still succeed rather than throwing', 'Handled a request with no session');
    ```

2. Show the right navigation for a visitor who is **not** logged in. Both blocks start hidden.

    ```html id=page1
    <div id="guest-navigation" style="display: none">Register or log in</div>
    <div id="user-info" style="display: none">Welcome back</div>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const loggedIn = false;

    // TODO: show '#guest-navigation' when loggedIn is false,
    // and '#user-info' when it is true, by setting style.display to 'block'
    ```
    ```+id=page1
    ```
    ```+solution
    const loggedIn = false;

    if (loggedIn) {
      document.querySelector('#user-info').style.display = 'block';
    } else {
      document.querySelector('#guest-navigation').style.display = 'block';
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(document.querySelector('#guest-navigation').style.display).should('equal', 'block').run('A visitor who is not logged in should see the guest navigation', 'Showed the guest navigation');
    wrap(document.querySelector('#user-info').style.display).should('equal', 'none').run('The welcome block should stay hidden for a visitor who is not logged in', 'Kept the welcome block hidden');
    wrap(/style\.display/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Reveal a block by setting its `style.display` to `block`', 'Used style.display');
    ```

3. Why does the logout response set `Max-Age=0` on the cookie?

    ```{mcq}
    What does that achieve?
    correct: Correct! It tells the browser the cookie has already expired, so the browser deletes it and stops sending the session ID.
    wrong: `Max-Age` controls how long a cookie lives. What does a lifetime of zero mean?
    [x] It makes the browser expire and delete the cookie immediately
    [ ] It keeps the cookie forever but hides it
    [ ] It raises the cookie's security level for the next login
    [ ] It is the default value for session cookies
    ```

4. Why delete the server's session as well as clearing the cookie? (Select all that apply)

    ```{mcq}
    What does deleting the server-side session accomplish? (Select all that apply)
    correct: Correct! Both. The session ID stops working even for someone who captured it earlier, and the server does not accumulate dead sessions for as long as it runs.
    wrong: Think about someone who copied the session ID before the logout, and separately about what happens to `sessions` over a week of logins.
    [x] An ID captured before the logout can no longer be used
    [x] The server stops holding session data it no longer needs
    [ ] It encrypts the database
    [ ] It logs the user out of other websites too
    ```

